WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.819
Film Courage: What was your first success
as a screenwriter?

00:00:01.819 --> 00:00:07.689
Gordy Hoffman, Bluecat Screenplay Founder/Screenwriter:
Well my first obvious success was LOVE, LIZA.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:19.141
Writing my second script I wrote was a drama
I wrote it in ’96 (the fall of ’96) and

00:00:19.141 --> 00:00:29.749
6 years later at Sundance it won at Sundance
the Waldo-Salt award and it was bought by

00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:34.180
Sony Classics and released by Sony and.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.760
That was definitely the first sort of obvious
success.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.800
I mean I had other things like options and
things but that was sort of like the conspicuous

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:59.490
success and my little brother I showed it
to him Labor Day Weekend 1996.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:04.789
We were both back at my mom’s house and
we both happened to be there and I said “Oh

00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:06.229
man you’ve got to read this.”

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:10.780
And I had just written the first draft and
I said “I think this is kind of good.”

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.109
And I said “I don’t know?

00:01:12.109 --> 00:01:13.600
I think this is kind of cool.”

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.399
He read it and he was like “I want to do
this!”

00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:20.420
And he had not shot BOOGIE NIGHTS yet.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.649
He was about ready to shoot BOOGIE NIGHTS
that fall.

00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:28.439
So that fall he shot BOOGIE NIGHTS with Paul
Thomas Anderson.

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:34.329
Paul Thomas Anderson read LOVE LIZA and actually
gave it to Michelle Satur at Sundance and

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:38.189
they considered it for the lab (the Sundance
lab).

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:39.229
So that was very exciting.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:44.200
I ended up moving out to LA in February of
’97 and then we started the process of trying

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.390
to get the movie made.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.810
Eventually we shot in 2001.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.899
Kathy Bates was in it.

00:01:56.899 --> 00:01:59.729
All that stuff felt successful.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:07.299
The idea that Sundance was interested and
it was great because I just had a short film

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:13.370
that I wrote and directed in 2015 and Michelle
Saturs is still there and she came up to me

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:19.040
and we still know each other from when she
called me on the phone and I was in Upstate

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:28.560
New York in the fall of ’96 and she interviewed
me about LOVE, LIZA and Paul had referred

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.349
the project to them and she said “Hey you
should check this out.”

00:02:31.349 --> 00:02:36.700
And she responded to the script, it didn’t
get in but they were always tracking it and

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.739
then obviously it was accepted.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:41.650
Paul’s movie HARD 8 was developed.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:47.220
It was originally called SYDNEY and his first
feature was developed through the Sundance

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.220
lab.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:54.920
So that was the relationship with the lab
that Paul had.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:02.300
So all that stuff was exciting but the most
popular thing that people would say is “Oh

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:08.500
Sundance, winning the award” Or whatever
but there was many other things that populated

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.260
my professional career that were like “Oh
this is exciting!”

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:17.409
Film Courage: When you sat down to write LOVE,
LIZA was that in your mind that it was going

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:20.080
to have a trajectory like that?

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:30.110
Gordy: Okay so I sat down to write LOVE, LIZA
and you know I had been driving a cab for

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:35.109
three and half years in Chicago and I had
just quit like a month before because I had

00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:42.379
sideswiped a limousine and I was like sort
of having this mental breakdown or whatever

00:03:42.379 --> 00:03:45.310
and I was just like “I just want to write.”

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.500
And my family gave me just a little bit of
rent money.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:53.340
So that summer I just didn’t work and I
was like I’ve got to do something and so

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.620
I remember August 5th (it was very clear),
I woke up and I had written 2 pages of it

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.610
and I wrote in like 18 days.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:04.030
I just sat there and wrote the whole thing.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:09.310
And I always wrote it with the sense that
I would play the guy…or not that I would

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:16.660
play the guy but I did write it with the sense
of “What if I was going to play the guy?”

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.810
And that really helped me as a writer you
know?

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.120
It was like it really got me.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.229
I’d never really done that much a lot since
then but I should probably think about the

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:31.210
sometimes because it was really effective
like “What if I play this guy?”

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:38.240
It was like this Orson Welles-ey sort of thing
like “I’m going to do everything.”

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.550
Like sort of when I was a kid.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:43.919
So I wrote it like that.

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:46.910
I didn’t write it for my brother.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:50.860
And I showed it to my brother because I was
like “This is good man!”

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.040
And I knew it was good.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:05:02.210
And I gave it to him at 10:30 at night and
he read it and the next day he was like “I

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.690
just read the whole thing.”

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:07.419
And he totally responded to it “I want to
do it.”

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:15.460
And I think it was like historically you look
back and I guess it was like two brothers

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:19.580
making this movie together but my brother
ended up being Phillip Seymour Hoffman and

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.690
doing all of these other things so historically
it looks like one thing but he actually was

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.530
just an actor who was like “I want to do
this role.”

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.319
Because it was a cool role, it was hard as
sh*t.

00:05:30.319 --> 00:05:32.940
But he liked it.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:39.470
And then we went on this journey together
and so yeah…I’m not sure what the question

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.569
was, but that’s what it was?

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:46.370
Film Courage: Did you expect this kind of
journey and it sounds like no that wasn’t

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:47.370
it?

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:54.190
Gordy: It was like no, you don’t expect
that sorts of things and then I didn’t expect

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:58.020
it would take so long to make it.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.120
There were so many things you were like “Oh
my gosh?”

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.849
And you didn’t expect it to not do all sorts
of things.

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:12.490
But we’re very lucky because we made a film
that there’s plenty of people that it’s

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.550
their favorite film.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:23.100
And I always tell people that the greatest
success is if you can write somebody’s favorite

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.720
film.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:33.000
If 
you write something and that’s their favorite

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.360
film it doesn’t get any better than that.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.819
You’re never going to get everybody to like
your film.

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:48.919
And the money is going to always disappear
and I still have people coming up to me and

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:53.750
are moved by the film, tear up when they talk
about it and go “Oh my God, you wrote LOVE

00:06:53.750 --> 00:07:01.600
LIZA and it means something to them and I’m
fortunate because that’s the kind of thing

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:10.360
that has guided me because it’s like nothing
else matters except giving your audience that

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:16.919
experience and if you get that to people and
some people will think it is crap or whatever

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:22.729
and some people will think it’s a nepotism
job or whatever or it’s like flimsy or whatever.

00:07:22.729 --> 00:07:27.630
It doesn’t matter because it’s a personal
experience, films are personal.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.569
There are plenty of people that I love CITIZEN
KANE.

00:07:30.569 --> 00:07:36.940
I think it genius and other people just hate
it.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.780
And it’s like there you go.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:43.259
Film Courage: Were you thinking about the
characters while you were driving (before

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:45.139
the limo accident)?

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:51.490
Gordy: I came up with the idea for LOVE LIZA
because when I was driving a cab I came up

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.030
with ideas all the time.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:08:02.870
And it was one of those ideas, I just saw
a woman, she was homeless, she was by the

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:10.910
gas pumps at a has station (as a taxi driver
you’re living at the gas station a lot,

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:13.009
you’re always there).

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:18.069
And I her saw her and it was like and it just
put in my mind this idea of a yuppie (which

00:08:18.069 --> 00:08:24.580
is not an acronym that is used anymore)…it’s
like a professional white guy in a suit suddenly

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:25.830
starting to huff gas.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.990
I just started seeing this guy huffing gas.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:34.479
That was the germ of the idea and then I had
to come up with a motive and then I came up

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:38.219
with the motive and I started writing it from
there.

00:08:38.219 --> 00:08:43.820
And then I started creating everything from
there and I came up with sort of a MacGuffiney

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:52.490
hook so to speak at around 10 minutes in the
movie and it’s that hook that sort of drove

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:56.579
and then like I said I just sort of splatted
it out.

00:08:56.579 --> 00:09:03.050
And honestly it probably could have been rewritten
a bunch more times.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:10.780
But it did get rewritten and I did fix a little
bit as a baby writer then so…that’s where

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.700
it came from.

